items.body:
## Summary

ContextBridge already records real execution durations and already keeps bounded relay-owned performance evidence per node/route/load profile.

This issue tracks a separate, explicitly **non-authoritative** layer:

> **Estimate remaining/total runtime from the pool's own historical evidence, and continuously recompute that estimate while work is running.**

This is intentionally different from #32:

- #32 = exact elapsed time from authoritative lifecycle timestamps.
- this issue = learned estimate from prior comparable executions.

The estimate must never be presented as guaranteed completion time, never become a routing/security authority by itself, and never degrade into "set ETA once and count down blindly".

## User-facing behavior

Bad:

```text
started · ETA 12m
... 11m
... 10m
... 9m
```

when the original 12m was only a guess.

Better:

```text
running · 04m12s elapsed
typical total · 08–13m · 27 comparable runs
est. remaining · 04–09m
```

Later, if the job exceeds the historical median:

```text
running · 11m08s elapsed
typical total · 08–13m
est. remaining · 01–06m · wider uncertainty
```

If it exceeds the useful historical range:

```text
running · 19m44s elapsed
outside typical range · estimate uncertain
```

The important behavior is that the displayed estimate changes because the evidence changed, **not** because a fixed deadline is merely counting down.

## Existing evidence to build on

Current CB already has:

- `Job.CreatedAt / AssignedAt / StartedAt / FinishedAt`;
- `Usage.ComputeMS / QueueMS`;
- selected node and durable routing decision;
- provider/model/task requirements;
- model warm/cold/unknown evidence at placement;
- bounded load classes;
- `RoutingPerformance` with samples, EWMA compute time and last completion;
- per-load-profile historical performance;
- bounded history TTL and minimum-sample controls.

The current placement documentation explicitly notes one limitation: historical performance does not yet normalize for prompt/token size.

This issue should reuse/extend the same evidence model rather than create a separate opaque predictor.

## Estimate hierarchy

Use the most specific bounded profile that has enough fresh samples, then fall back.

Candidate order:

1. stable operator-owned pipeline step + selected node/route + comparable input/load class;
2. selected node/route + comparable input/load class;
3. selected node/route baseline;
4. provider/model/task pool baseline;
5. no estimate.

Never fabricate an estimate merely to avoid showing "unknown".

A future producer-specific deviation profile may be useful, but only under a bounded-cardinality design and only after enough evidence. Do not key an unbounded metric table directly by arbitrary user/job/session IDs.

## Workload normalization

A duration estimate becomes much more useful when it distinguishes materially different workload sizes.

Candidate bounded input classes include only facts CB already legitimately knows, for example:

- input token-count bucket when known;
- requested/actual output-token bucket when known;
- image count and decoded-byte bucket;
- document count;
- artifact/input byte bucket;
- task kind;
- selected model/runtime;
- warm/cold/unknown model state;
- assignment load class.

Do not persist prompts/content merely to improve timing prediction.

Unknown values stay unknown; do not infer token count from text length and then present it as measured fact unless clearly labelled as an estimate.

## Pipeline-step learning

Stable CB-owned step IDs can be valuable evidence because the same step often represents comparable work.

For configured deterministic pipelines, a bounded key can include:

```text
pipeline identity / step identity / route class
```

provided:

- the pipeline/step comes from operator-owned bounded configuration;
- retained profile count is capped;
- renamed/deleted steps age out normally;
- step history cannot widen permissions or force routing.

For future DAGs (#29), each branch can learn independently.

For current CLI bounded agents, do not treat free plan/source strings as a durable first-class step-performance namespace until #33's explicit run/correlation boundary exists.

## Distribution, not one average

An EWMA is useful for placement, but a user-facing completion estimate needs uncertainty.

Keep bounded distribution evidence such as one of:

- fixed histogram buckets;
- bounded recent successful-duration reservoir;
- another deterministic bounded quantile structure.

The implementation should be able to expose at least a typical range such as p50/p90 or equivalent.

Do not retain an unbounded sample list.

Failed/cancelled/ambiguous runs should not be mixed into the successful-completion duration distribution. They may have their own reliability evidence elsewhere.

## Dynamic remaining-time estimate

Once execution starts, remaining time should be conditioned on the fact that the job has **already survived for the current elapsed duration**.

Conceptually:

```text
historical total duration distribution = D
elapsed = e

remaining distribution ~= (D - e) conditioned on D > e
```

The implementation does not need an academic survival-analysis package, but it should preserve that intuition.

At minimum:

- recompute from current elapsed time;
- never show a negative remaining duration;
- once elapsed passes a historical quantile, drop samples/regions that would already have completed;
- widen or withdraw the estimate when too little comparable evidence remains;
- do not pin a single absolute finish timestamp at job start.

## Re-estimation triggers

The display can be recomputed locally on a bounded cadence without durable events.

Evidence may legitimately change when:

- the selected node becomes known;
- execution actually starts;
- exact model/runtime becomes known;
- measured token/output counts become available;
- advisory structured progress arrives from the current worker;
- elapsed time crosses the learned distribution.

If optional worker progress is incorporated later, keep the source visible:

```text
estimate from history
estimate adjusted by worker-reported 64% progress (advisory)
```

Worker progress never becomes authoritative completion.

## Confidence / evidence display

Prefer transparent evidence over false precision.

Examples:

```text
est. 4–9m remaining · 27 samples
est. ~6m remaining · low confidence · 4 samples
estimate unavailable · insufficient comparable history
```

Possible confidence inputs:

- sample count;
- evidence age;
- spread/variance;
- specificity of matched profile;
- whether the job has moved outside the historical range.

Do not turn confidence into a marketing-style percentage unless the statistical meaning is actually defined.

## Privacy / local-first boundary

This learning should be local to the user's CB deployment by default.

No central telemetry or cross-customer timing dataset is required.

Historical keys must avoid:

- prompt/result content;
- tenant names in public metrics;
- arbitrary session IDs;
- raw adapter session identifiers;
- high-cardinality error text.

The relay may use opaque bounded route/workload fingerprints where needed.

## Storage and cardinality

Hard requirements:

- bounded profiles per node/route;
- bounded workload-size classes;
- bounded retained distribution state per profile;
- TTL/aging;
- deterministic eviction;
- no `user × node × model × step × arbitrary input` Cartesian explosion.

Prometheus remains fixed-cardinality. Rich per-route/per-step learned evidence belongs in bounded relay state/API diagnostics, not as unbounded metric labels.

## Routing interaction

This feature may eventually improve placement, but UI estimation and routing authority should remain separable.

Any routing use must retain today's rules:

1. hard permissions/capabilities/policy first;
2. current capacity/resource evidence next;
3. historical performance only as bounded soft evidence.

A timing predictor must never make an otherwise ineligible worker eligible.

## API / projection

A future machine-readable estimate could resemble:

```json
{
  "kind": "historical_runtime",
  "status": "available",
  "samples": 27,
  "profile": "node_route_load_input",
  "elapsed_ms": 252000,
  "total_p50_ms": 510000,
  "total_p90_ms": 780000,
  "remaining_p50_ms": 258000,
  "remaining_p90_ms": 528000,
  "evidence_age_seconds": 420
}
```

Exact schema TBD.

Formatted strings such as `04–09m` belong in CLI/UI, not as the only API representation.

## Relationship to other issues

- #35 tracks a concrete freshness defect in the first duration-reservoir implementation: one new completion can currently make older-than-TTL samples eligible again. #34 should not be considered complete until that freshness invariant is fixed and regression-tested.
- #32 owns exact elapsed-time presentation.
- #33 owns bounded active-work grouping.
- #29 owns DAG execution semantics.
- #16 owns authoritative/advisory progress semantics.

A useful future activity row may therefore show:

```text
● render-a · running · 01h23m · est. 12–28m remaining
```

where:

- `running` and `01h23m` are authoritative;
- `12–28m remaining` is explicitly historical estimate.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] no estimate is shown without sufficient fresh comparable evidence;
- [ ] estimate is labelled as estimated/non-authoritative;
- [ ] estimate uses a bounded duration distribution, not only one EWMA;
- [ ] active remaining-time estimate is recomputed as elapsed time advances;
- [ ] it does not simply count down from one start-time guess;
- [ ] exceeding historical p50/p90 updates/widens/withdraws the estimate rather than going negative;
- [ ] stable pipeline steps can build bounded local history;
- [ ] workload-size normalization uses bounded metadata classes, not content retention;
- [ ] storage/cardinality remain explicitly bounded;
- [ ] failed/cancelled/ambiguous runs do not contaminate successful-duration quantiles;
- [ ] reconnect reproduces the same estimate from retained evidence + authoritative timestamps;
- [ ] no periodic durable timer events are needed;
- [ ] routing hard gates remain unchanged;
- [ ] tests cover sparse history, stale history, outliers, long-running beyond p90, and dynamic re-estimation.

## Non-goals

- guaranteed ETA;
- cloud-wide fleet telemetry;
- training a black-box model on prompt contents;
- using timing estimates as security/policy authority;
- pretending unknown workloads are predictable.

The goal is a pool that gradually becomes better at answering:

> **"On my actual machines, with this kind of work, what usually happens?"**

without confusing experience with certainty.

## Context

TXO Fabric should eventually support a real-time voice channel between a human and an AgentIdentity.

The target is not “upload an audio file and get a transcript”. The v1.0 capability should support a natural spoken conversation:

- streaming speech-to-text (STT);
- streaming text-to-speech (TTS);
- low enough latency for conversational turn-taking;
- **barge-in / interruption** while the agent is speaking;
- robust behavior with imperfect microphones and noisy environments such as a moving car, road noise, ventilation, speakerphone, Bluetooth headsets or mediocre mobile audio.

This is a **v1.0 product capability**, not required for the current v0/v0.1 Fabric baseline.

## Product goal

An authenticated human can speak naturally to an authorized Fabric AgentIdentity and hear a synthesized response, while preserving stable agent identity, tenant isolation, session isolation and the existing Fabric authorization model.

The experience must remain usable when audio quality is materially worse than a quiet desktop microphone.

## Core interaction contract

Conceptually:

```
microphone
   ↓
capture / preprocessing
   ↓
VAD / speech segmentation
   ↓
streaming STT
   ↓
Fabric human/agent routing
   ↓
AgentIdentity / Hermes
   ↓
streaming response
   ↓
streaming TTS
   ↓
speaker
```

Voice is a transport/channel. It must not redefine AgentIdentity, memory, credentials or IAM ownership.

## Barge-in / interruption

Interruption is a first-class requirement.

While TTS audio is playing, new detected user speech must be able to:

1. stop/cancel the currently playing synthesized audio quickly;
2. stop or cancel the corresponding TTS generation where supported;
3. decide whether the underlying LLM generation should also be cancelled or whether partial generation can be retained;
4. start STT for the interrupting speech;
5. continue the same logical conversation without duplicating or replaying stale output.

The implementation must explicitly handle races such as:

- user starts speaking while the last TTS chunk is buffered;
- STT false-positive caused by the agent audio itself;
- repeated interruption/restart;
- network-delayed audio chunks arriving after cancellation;
- user interrupts before the model response has completed.

“Mute local playback but continue everything server-side” is not sufficient as the only behavior.

## Adverse acoustic conditions

The acceptance target must include **realistic poor-quality audio**, not only clean studio samples.

Representative cases:

- moving car / road noise;
- ventilation / fan noise;
- Bluetooth headset;
- speakerphone;
- low-cost laptop/mobile microphone;
- varying speech volume;
- short dropouts / packet jitter;
- moderate reverberation;
- agent TTS leaking back into the microphone.

Evaluate where appropriate:

- acoustic echo cancellation (AEC);
- noise suppression / denoising;
- automatic gain control (AGC);
- voice activity detection (VAD);
- endpointing;
- microphone/speaker device handling;
- WebRTC audio processing or equivalent;
- STT model/provider robustness against noise.

Do not assume that one STT model alone solves the acoustic pipeline.

## STT requirements

Evaluate and implement a provider-neutral STT boundary supporting at least:

- streaming / partial transcription where the backend permits it;
- final transcript events;
- language selection / auto-detection policy;
- confidence/quality signals where available;
- punctuation/normalization policy;
- cancellation;
- timeout/reconnect behavior;
- explicit failure state;
- tenant/agent/session correlation;
- optional local/self-hosted and remote/provider-backed implementations.

The Fabric-facing contract should not expose provider-specific semantics unnecessarily.

## TTS requirements

Evaluate and implement a provider-neutral TTS boundary supporting at least:

- streaming audio output where supported;
- low first-audio latency;
- cancellation / barge-in;
- voice selection policy;
- language/locale;
- rate/speed controls where justified;
- explicit failure/degraded mode;
- tenant/agent/session correlation;
- optional local/self-hosted and remote/provider-backed implementations.

Voice/persona selection belongs to product/runtime policy, not arbitrary untrusted prompt content.

## Full-duplex / audio architecture

Investigate the appropriate transport, including at least:

- browser/mobile WebRTC;
- WebSocket/streaming HTTP where appropriate;
- server-side audio session broker;
- direct provider realtime APIs where compatible with Fabric isolation;
- hybrid models where STT/TTS are separated from LLM inference.

The chosen design must preserve Fabric policy enforcement and observability.

Avoid binding the entire voice architecture to one provider's proprietary realtime API unless there is an explicit abstraction/fallback strategy.

## Identity, authorization and routing

Voice must reuse the existing human-entry principles:

- human identity != AgentIdentity;
- authorization happens before access to the target AgentIdentity;
- one human may be authorized for several agents;
- voice session routing uses stable Fabric identity, not Pod names/IPs;
- no cross-agent or cross-tenant audio/session leakage;
- reconnect/restart preserves logical target identity where the session contract permits it.

This issue should relate to the Fabric tenant entrypoint / future multi-channel boundary rather than creating a parallel identity model.

## Memory / transcript semantics

Define explicitly:

- whether final STT transcript is inserted into the normal conversation as the user's message;
- whether partial transcripts are ephemeral only;
- whether synthesized output is reconstructed from the canonical model text or stored independently;
- whether raw audio is retained at all;
- retention/consent policy for audio recordings;
- interaction with Hindsight/memory retention;
- redaction policy for logs and traces.

Default direction: do not persist raw audio merely because the transport is voice.

## Privacy / security

Voice may contain sensitive customer/user data.

Define:

- whether raw audio can leave the infrastructure;
- which provider receives audio/transcript/text;
- tenant policy for offshore/third-party processing;
- encryption in transit;
- temporary buffering and deletion;
- observability without storing raw conversations;
- secret/credential handling;
- abuse/rate limits;
- maximum session duration;
- explicit handling of accidental background audio.

Provider choice must remain compatible with the product's privacy/RGPD positioning.

## Latency / quality objectives

Establish measurable service objectives rather than “feels realtime”.

At minimum measure:

- time from speech start to first partial STT;
- end-of-speech to final transcript;
- final transcript to first agent token;
- first agent token/text to first TTS audio;
- interruption speech detected to TTS playback stopped;
- recovery time after interruption;
- transcript quality under representative noisy conditions.

Do not hard-code final numeric SLOs in this issue before benchmarking, but the implementation PR must define and justify targets.

## Degraded modes

Define deterministic behavior when:

- STT provider is unavailable;
- TTS provider is unavailable;
- network quality degrades;
- microphone permission is denied;
- audio device disappears;
- VAD repeatedly misfires;
- TTS cancellation fails;
- LLM response continues after a voice-session disconnect.

Potential degraded modes may include:

- fall back to text input/output;
- retry/reconnect;
- continue transcript-only;
- explicit session termination.

No silent routing to a different tenant/agent/provider policy.

## Provider / implementation evaluation

Before selecting a production implementation, compare viable options for:

- self-hosted STT;
- hosted STT;
- self-hosted TTS;
- hosted TTS;
- realtime end-to-end speech models;
- WebRTC/media-session infrastructure;
- denoising/AEC/VAD stack.

Evaluation criteria:

- noisy-speech accuracy;
- latency;
- interruption support;
- French quality first, then multilingual extensibility;
- self-hosting feasibility;
- GPU/CPU cost;
- per-minute provider cost;
- privacy / residency;
- operational complexity;
- licensing;
- streaming API quality;
- observability;
- failover/provider abstraction.

The issue intentionally does **not** preselect Whisper, Deepgram, ElevenLabs, OpenAI Realtime, local Piper/Kokoro/etc.; choose based on current benchmark and product requirements when implementation begins.


## Deployment topology: shared pool vs per-tenant speech stack

The v1.0 design must explicitly decide the deployment/isolation model for STT/TTS. Do not assume that speech infrastructure follows the same tenancy model as AgentIdentity runtimes.

Evaluate at least:

### A. Shared Fabric speech pool

One or more shared STT/TTS services serve several tenants.

Potential advantages:

- better GPU/CPU utilization;
- lower idle cost;
- easier model warming/caching;
- fewer replicas/models to operate;
- simpler upgrades and provider failover.

Risks / required controls:

- strict tenant/session isolation;
- per-tenant quotas and rate limits;
- fair scheduling / noisy-neighbor protection;
- no cross-tenant transcript/audio leakage;
- tenant-tagged observability;
- blast radius of service failure;
- provider credentials/policy separation where tenants differ;
- data residency/privacy policy compatibility.

### B. Dedicated speech stack per tenant

Each tenant receives dedicated STT/TTS runtime capacity or dedicated provider bindings.

Potential advantages:

- strong operational/isolation boundary;
- easier tenant-specific provider/model/residency policy;
- simpler per-tenant accounting;
- failure/noise containment.

Costs / risks:

- poor utilization for low-volume tenants;
- high GPU memory/idle cost;
- more upgrade/patching surface;
- model duplication;
- potentially slower cold starts;
- operational complexity at tenant scale.

### C. Hybrid model

Evaluate a hybrid architecture, for example:

- shared inference workers/model pool;
- tenant-isolated queues/sessions/credentials/policies;
- optional dedicated pool/profile for high-volume, regulated or premium tenants;
- configurable `SpeechRuntimeProfile` / equivalent policy selecting shared vs dedicated execution;
- common control plane with tenant-scoped data plane semantics.

This is likely to offer the best cost/isolation trade-off, but must be proven rather than assumed.

### Decision criteria

Benchmark/compare the topologies against:

- GPU/CPU utilization and idle cost;
- concurrency and queue latency;
- first-audio latency / model warm state;
- noisy-neighbor behavior;
- per-tenant quotas/budgets;
- tenant-specific language/model/voice policy;
- privacy/RGPD/data residency;
- provider-key isolation;
- audit/accounting granularity;
- blast radius;
- horizontal scaling;
- upgrade/canary strategy;
- failover;
- operational complexity;
- support for dedicated capacity as a commercial tier.

### Required outcome

The v1.0 architecture must document whether speech is:

- globally shared,
- shared per tenant,
- dedicated per tenant,
- or hybrid/configurable,

and define the isolation contract accordingly.

The public Fabric API must remain topology-neutral: AgentIdentity/human clients should request a speech capability/session, not address a specific STT/TTS pod or provider.


## Validation strategy

### Automated

Where practical, maintain a reproducible audio corpus containing:

- clean speech;
- road/car noise mixed at several SNR levels;
- fan/ventilation noise;
- reverberant speech;
- simulated TTS echo;
- short interruptions/barge-in utterances.

Tests should verify:

- expected transcript semantics;
- no cross-session leakage;
- interruption state machine;
- cancellation of stale audio;
- deterministic routing;
- provider failure/degraded behavior.

Synthetic/noise-mixed audio is useful for regression, but does not replace physical testing.

### Physical acceptance

Test with real hardware/environments including at least:

- quiet desktop;
- mediocre laptop/mobile microphone;
- Bluetooth headset;
- speakerphone;
- **real moving-car or realistic road-noise scenario**.

Acceptance must include live interruption while the agent is speaking.

## Suggested implementation slices

1. **Voice session contract**
   - session identity/routing;
   - transport;
   - event/state model;
   - cancellation semantics.

2. **Streaming STT abstraction**
   - provider adapter(s);
   - VAD/endpointing;
   - noisy-audio benchmark.

3. **Streaming TTS abstraction**
   - provider adapter(s);
   - streaming playback;
   - cancellation.

4. **Barge-in state machine**
   - echo-safe user-speech detection;
   - generation/TTS cancellation;
   - stale-chunk rejection.

5. **Browser/mobile reference client**
   - audio capture;
   - device permissions;
   - reconnect;
   - fallback to text.

6. **Privacy/retention policy**
   - raw audio;
   - transcripts;
   - metrics/logs.

7. **Physical v1.0 acceptance**
   - quiet + noisy/car scenarios;
   - latency/quality report;
   - multi-agent authorization proof.

## Non-goals

- PSTN/telephone gateway in the first slice unless separately accepted;
- call-center routing/queues;
- voice cloning;
- emotion inference as an authorization/business signal;
- permanent raw-audio recording by default;
- replacing the text/web channel;
- coupling AgentIdentity directly to one STT/TTS vendor.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A provider-neutral voice session architecture is documented.
- [ ] Streaming STT works against the chosen reference implementation.
- [ ] Streaming TTS works against the chosen reference implementation.
- [ ] Final STT text enters the intended AgentIdentity conversation with no cross-agent leakage.
- [ ] One authorized human can choose between at least two AgentIdentity targets.
- [ ] Live barge-in stops audible TTS promptly and prevents stale cancelled audio from resuming.
- [ ] Interruption semantics for model generation vs TTS generation are explicit and tested.
- [ ] Echo from agent playback does not routinely trigger false user interruption.
- [ ] Voice remains usable in a representative moving-car/road-noise scenario.
- [ ] Quiet, noisy, Bluetooth and speakerphone test results are recorded.
- [ ] Latency metrics are instrumented and target SLOs are documented from measurements.
- [ ] STT/TTS provider failure has an explicit degraded/fallback behavior.
- [ ] Raw-audio retention policy is explicit and privacy/RGPD impact documented.
- [ ] No provider credentials or raw audio leak into AgentIdentity persistent state/shared workspaces by default.
- [ ] Voice routing survives AgentIdentity Pod replacement.
- [ ] Automated regression audio corpus/state-machine tests exist.
- [ ] Physical end-to-end acceptance includes actual barge-in and noisy audio.

## Related architecture

- #3689 — Fabric human-entry boundary
- #3806 — stable tenant/agent public routing namespace
- #3807 — future tenant-facing Fabric entrypoint / multi-channel product boundary
- #3808 — private implementation-service boundary

## Target

**v1.0**

Voice is intentionally deferred beyond the current v0.x baseline, but the architecture should remain compatible with it when human-entry and multi-channel contracts evolve.

## Purpose
Advance the original AIIBP intelligence roadmap now, while the remaining platform domains are completed, so Python AI does not arrive after the data architecture is already fixed.

## Deadline context
Hard production-completion target: **30 November 2026**, including Python AI. Platform feature-complete target remains 19 November; Python AI feature-complete target remains 26 November; 27–30 November is reserved for final hardening and production acceptance.

## Architectural rule
Operational Node/Express/MongoDB domains remain authoritative. Python/FastAPI supplies intelligence only. AI may read governed snapshots/evidence and return predictions/recommendations/proposals, but it must not become booking/customer/RBAC/tenant authority or directly mutate consequential canonical state.

## Immediate implementation scope
1. Add trusted Business/Location/vertical provenance to the existing AI feature, inference and dataset governance records without breaking current single-tenant behaviour.
2. Keep `AiFeatureSnapshot` time-aware and pseudonymised by default; explicitly carry tenant/location provenance so future model training cannot mix tenants accidentally.
3. Extend `AiInferenceLog` with trusted tenant/location/vertical provenance so evaluation, drift, outcomes and feedback remain isolated.
4. Extend `AiTrainingDataset` with an explicit dataset scope contract supporting single-Business, governed cross-Business de-identified, and platform/global datasets.
5. Preserve existing global model-version governance; do not duplicate `AiModelVersion` or create a second AI registry.
6. Add regression tests for the scope defaults and tenant provenance contract.
7. Update AI architecture documentation to require governed dataset snapshots rather than direct training from raw production collections.

## Next increments after this foundation
- tenant-aware AI query/evaluation scoping;
- governed dataset builder from canonical tenant-safe operational domains;
- deterministic de-identification/feature extraction;
- data-quality/readiness report and frozen dataset snapshot;
- Python training CLI/pipeline with temporal splits and baseline evaluation;
- no-show model first, followed by consultation/recommendation workloads;
- model registry/artifact/evaluation integration;
- tenant-aware production inference and outcome feedback;
- consultation engine and later specialist AI Workforce agents through the existing proposal/approval/audit architecture.

## Guardrails
- no raw production training dump;
- no direct identifiers by default;
- no cross-tenant mixing without an explicit governed dataset scope;
- no autonomous consequential writes;
- no duplicate CRM/booking/RBAC/AI authority;
- no production migration at startup;
- CI/security/CodeQL/backend smoke must remain green.

Refs #237 #307 #280 #276 and the existing `AiTrainingDataset`, `AiFeatureSnapshot`, `AiInferenceLog`, `AiModelVersion` governance foundation.

## Problem

Reborn already routes `builtin.shell` through Docker for explicit user-sandbox profiles, but the current implementation is not the persistent user computer we want to ship:

- local Docker creates and removes a container for every shell command;
- `/workspace` persists per `(tenant, user)`, but installed packages, background services, `/tmp`, `$HOME`, and process state do not;
- sandbox profiles enable unrestricted direct network egress through `RebornSandboxConfig::with_network_enabled()`;
- the default worker image is Python-only;
- network allowlist, placeholder, CA, and credential-firewall pieces are not wired into a production proxy path.

The product decision is now explicit: **one persistent sandbox per user**. Threads and turns share that user's computer. IronClaw remains the trusted control plane outside it.

This supersedes earlier issue comments and body text proposing per-command, per-run, or per-thread containers.

## Decisions — settled 2026-08-19

### D1. IronClaw remains the trusted control plane

The agent loop, process journal, identity, authorization, approvals, secret store, model calls, durable events, and exit validation stay host-side.

```text
IronClaw host
  CanonicalAgentLoopExecutor
    -> CapabilityHost
    -> builtin.shell
    -> UserSandboxProcessPort
    -> persistent per-user sandbox
```

The sandbox may request authority. It cannot mint authority, access the Docker socket, choose host paths, or receive real credentials.

### D2. Granularity: sandbox, proxy, network, and workspace per user

```text
per (tenant, user), long-lived:
  - one persistent sandbox container
  - one persistent private Docker network
  - one iron-proxy sidecar
  - one durable /workspace and persistent user environment

per command:
  - docker exec inside the user container
  - bounded process group, output, and deadline

per invocation:
  - placeholder credential values
  - approved destination/account/scope bindings
  - proxy config entries and expiry
```

Why per user:

- it matches the product promise: every user gets one computer;
- tools, packages, daemons, and local configuration remain available across threads and days;
- 100 users means roughly 100 user sandboxes, not one sandbox per historical thread;
- `docker exec` avoids per-command container/network churn;
- cross-thread collaboration happens through one intentional workspace rather than synchronization between thread machines.

Accepted trade-off: a process or poisoned toolchain can affect later threads for the same user. Cross-user isolation, real-secret custody, host-side policy, idle recycle, reset, and invocation-scoped credentials bound that risk. Stronger per-run/per-thread isolation remains a later mode where workloads require it.

### D3. User-container lifecycle

```text
ABSENT -> ACTIVE -> IDLE/STOPPED -> ACTIVE
             |             |
             +-- recycle --+
```

V1 implementation:

- `ensure_user_container(scope)` keyed by typed tenant/user identity;
- adopt a compatible running container after IronClaw restart;
- start a compatible stopped container;
- recycle on image digest or security-posture mismatch;
- serialize create/adopt/recycle per user key;
- execute commands with Docker Exec;
- stop only when active-exec count is zero;
- reap/remove stopped containers after configurable retention while preserving `/workspace`;
- `shutdown()` stops host supervisors but leaves compatible containers adoptable.

`docker pause`/memory snapshot and declarative service restart are deferred. Background processes survive while the user container remains active; STOPPED/recycled containers lose processes but keep the user workspace and persistent home/tool directories.

### D4. Process supervision

The worker image contains:

- `tini` as PID 1 to reap children;
- `ironclaw-exec` to run a command in its own session/process group;
- bounded TERM -> KILL handling on timeout;
- exact exit-code propagation and bounded stdout/stderr.

Bollard cannot reliably terminate an individual Docker Exec process tree by dropping a host future. The in-container supervisor is required.

### D5. Egress: one per-user iron-proxy, no direct route

The user container and its proxy share a private internal network. The proxy is dual-homed to approved upstream egress. Thread attribution is unnecessary because one proxy belongs to one user.

- remove `with_network_enabled()` from sandbox profiles;
- direct public/private/metadata egress from the user container must fail;
- upstream `ironsh/iron-proxy` is pinned by digest and security-reviewed per bump;
- static default-deny allowlist comes from host-owned `NetworkPolicy`;
- private, loopback, link-local, metadata, and DNS-rebinding destinations remain denied;
- proxy management API is host-only;
- proxy/policy failure never falls back to direct egress;
- no LLM judge participates in authorization.

The Step 0 spike proved the topology, Docker DNS forwarding, direct-route denial, MITM trust, structured denials, placeholder replacement, and runtime CA requirements. Evidence: `docs/internal/research/2026-08-19-sandbox-egress-spike/`.

### D6. Credentials: placeholders in the sandbox, real values only at the edge

iron-proxy already provides the swap mechanism. IronClaw owns policy and lifecycle:

- mint high-entropy invocation-scoped placeholders;
- render host/location/account/scope bindings from satisfied `CapabilityHost` obligations;
- inject placeholders through CLI-required env/files only for the invocation;
- keep real credential material in the host/proxy boundary only;
- remove expired bindings through config reload;
- use `require: true` and exact location binding;
- run credential-bearing commands through direct argv (`ValidatedSandboxProcessPlan`), not `sh -c`;
- generalize credential consumers so a sandbox process does not pretend to be an extension;
- default new command identities to digest-bound one-shot approval.

A resident process in the user's sandbox must not be able to reuse a different invocation's placeholder. If proxy scoping cannot provide that guarantee under concurrent commands, credential-bearing commands use an ephemeral sibling runner while sharing the user's workspace read/write and tools read-only.

### D7. Worker image and persistent environment

Pinned baseline image:

- `bash`, `git`, `curl`, `ca-certificates`, `jq`;
- Python;
- Node.js/npm;
- GitHub CLI;
- `tini`, `setsid`, `ironclaw-exec`;
- proxy CA trust configuration (`NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS`, `SSL_CERT_FILE`, system trust store).

Persistent user-controlled state must live on durable mounts (`/workspace`, selected `$HOME`/tool directories). Rootfs remains read-only and replaceable. Heavy/cloud-specific CLIs remain optional pinned images. No credential is baked into an image.

### D8. Scale model

Total historical threads do not create more sandboxes. The cardinality is users:

```text
100 users -> at most 100 user sandbox bundles
active work is bounded by runner workers and per-user concurrency limits
idle users can be stopped/reaped while their durable workspace remains
```

Required controls:

- global and per-user active-exec quotas;
- memory/CPU/PID/output/deadline ceilings;
- idle stop and retention reaper;
- label-filtered lookups, never full-daemon scans;
- lazy posture/image recycle to avoid deployment storms;
- clear disk/workspace quota and reset behavior;
- an explicit per-user concurrency policy for commands sharing one filesystem.

### D9. Docker now; remote/microVM later behind kept seams

Docker is the initial substrate because the control plane and containers run on one Linux host and it is available to self-hosters. Cloud PaaS products without a Docker daemon are not suitable for this shape.

Keep the later transition cheap:

- sandbox lifecycle behind a provider/transport boundary;
- no model-supplied host paths or Docker flags;
- filesystem access through mount contracts, not hardcoded host assumptions;
- future provisioner shape: create / attach volume / pause / resume / destroy.

A microVM or remote sandbox backend becomes justified by a concrete isolation, density, enterprise-cloud, or remote-compute requirement. It does not block V1.

### D10. Per-user egress allowlists live in durable settings, not an env var

Each SSO user controls which public hosts their own sandbox may reach. The env var (`IRONCLAW_SANDBOX_EXTRA_ALLOWED_DOMAINS`) remains a host-wide bootstrap knob only; user-editable allowlists live in the persisted settings layer keyed by `(tenant, user)` so they survive restarts and are editable through the product.

Resolution, computed per user at `ensure_bundle` time:

```text
effective_allowlist(user) = global defaults
                          ∪ tenant extras (operator-owned)
                          ∪ user extras   (settings, approval-gated)
                          − tenant deny ceiling (operator-owned, wins over everything)
```

Why the plumbing is already shaped for this (Phase 2):

- the proxy, its private network, and its rendered `proxy.yaml` are already per user;
- the posture stamp hashes the rendered policy, so an allowlist edit changes the user's posture and the existing incompatible-proxy recycle path rebuilds their proxy transparently on the next command — no restart, no reload plumbing;
- validation is the same fail-closed pipeline the env var uses today (exact hostnames only, no wildcards, non-empty labels).

Non-negotiable invariants, out of user reach:

- **the model must never widen its own allowlist unattended** — edits happen only through the Settings surface or an explicit approval gate; any model-callable mutation requires a human approval per change;
- deny-CIDRs (loopback, private, link-local, metadata, documentation ranges) apply at resolved-IP level regardless of any user entry;
- wildcards and raw-IP entries are rejected for user extras exactly as for the env var;
- tenant admins keep a deny ceiling user entries cannot escape;
- every proxied request stays in the per-user audit trail, including which allowlist source admitted it.

LLM data-retention rule applies: allowlist edits are recorded as settings history (timestamped, filterable), never destructive overwrites.

