## Implementation phases

### Phase 1 — persistent per-user container + Docker Exec (#7764)

- reuse `RebornSandboxUserKey` for stable container identity;
- one container per `(tenant,user)`, shared across threads;
- persistent per-user workspace;
- ensure/adopt/start/recycle with per-user serialization;
- `tini` + `ironclaw-exec`;
- active-exec guard, idle stop, restart/adoption;
- missing thread id is allowed because identity is user-scoped;
- no network behavior changes in this phase.

Acceptance:

- same user across different threads gets the same container and workspace;
- concurrent first calls across threads converge on one user container;
- different users/tenants get different containers/workspaces;
- restart adopts, stopped restarts, posture/image mismatch recycles;
- timeout leaves no descendants; non-zero exit remains ordinary output;
- idle stop never interrupts active exec;
- full native-loop turn remains green.

### Phase 2 — per-user iron-proxy + no direct egress

- persistent per-user private network + proxy sidecar;
- container routes only through proxy;
- render static policy from host grants;
- remove direct-network posture;
- lifecycle/adoption/cleanup for proxy and network;
- worker CA trust across curl/git/Node/Python.

Acceptance:

- allowlisted credential-free requests work;
- denied host returns 403 and never reaches upstream;
- direct public/private/metadata/raw-IP attempts fail;
- proxy audit is correlated to user/invocation;
- management API is unreachable from sandbox;
- idle/reap behavior does not leave credential-bearing orphan proxies.

### Phase 3 — credentialed CLI path

- one vendor canary first (GitHub);
- placeholders + `require: true` + location binding;
- direct-argv process plan executor;
- sandbox-process credential consumer;
- digest-bound approvals and expiry;
- durable redacted audit.

### Phase 4 — curated image + operator surface

- publish/provision pinned worker/proxy images;
- baseline CLI/CA smoke tests;
- readiness reports image/proxy/direct-egress posture;
- disk quota, idle/retention, and reset controls documented.

### Phase 5 — canary and close

Prove credential-free, credentialed read, approved mutation + read-back, timeout, restart, concurrent threads for one user, concurrent users, denied scope/host, and leak-negative scenarios.

### Phase 6 — per-user egress allowlists (D10)

Builds on Phase 2; independent of Phases 3–5.

- persisted `(tenant, user)` allowlist setting with the shared hostname-shape validation; dual-backend parity via the settings conformance suites;
- policy resolution moves from transport construction to per-user `ensure_bundle`; posture stamp covers the resolved per-user policy so edits recycle the proxy lazily;
- Settings UI (and optional approval-gated model tool) for add/remove; every mutation approval-gated and audited;
- tenant ceiling: operator deny-list evaluated after the union; conflicts resolve closed;
- Railway parity: per-user config render replaces the construction-time template;
- env var demoted to bootstrap-only defaults; documented in `.env.example`.

Acceptance:

- user A adds `api.example.com`, next command reaches it through their proxy; user B is still denied;
- removing an entry recycles the proxy and the host is denied on the next command;
- a model-initiated allowlist mutation without human approval is rejected;
- a user entry matching the tenant deny ceiling or a deny-CIDR resolution is refused at write time or denied at connect time respectively;
- audit records name the admitting source (default / tenant / user entry).

## Deferred options

| Option | Trigger |
|---|---|
| Per-thread/per-run ephemeral isolation mode | workloads require stronger same-user cross-thread isolation |
| Ephemeral credential sibling runner | per-user proxy + invocation placeholder cannot prevent concurrent resident-process reuse |
| Request-time hold-and-approve | grant-time approval proves too coarse |
| Sandbox-authoritative filesystem/executor daemon | remote backend or microVM removes host bind mount |
| CLI harness wrappers / second `TurnRunExecutor` | comparative eval proves external harness value |
| MicroVM provider | isolation/density/enterprise requirement |
| Docker-in-Docker | a concrete workflow needs nested Docker; prefer Sysbox or microVM rather than host socket/privileged container |

## Accepted trade-offs

- Same-user threads intentionally share one process environment and filesystem.
- A poisoned package/background process can influence later commands for that user until recycle/reset.
- Workspace/toolchain poisoning persists across container recreation; reset/snapshot and provenance address it.
- Grant-time HTTP authorization is coarser than typed capabilities until request-time approval exists.
- The blast radius of a compromised user sandbox is that user's current sandbox and live authorized invocations — never another user, the host secret store, or durable policy authority.

## Required validation

- unit coverage for user key/adoption/activity/output/deadline logic;
- real-Docker tests for cross-thread reuse, cross-user isolation, concurrent startup, restart adoption, stopped restart, posture recycle, idle stop, process-group timeout, and cleanup;
- full-turn integration through `builtin.shell`;
- architecture tests for dependency/placement rules;
- Step 2 negative network/DNS/metadata/credential-location tests;
- worker image and proxy CA trust smoke tests;
- no raw secrets in env/files/output/logs/events.

## Close criteria

Close when an explicit sandbox profile gives every user one persistent, reusable, proxy-enforced computer; shell/CLI state persists across threads; real credentials never enter it; direct egress is structurally blocked; lifecycle and quotas scale by users rather than thread count; failure paths clean up deterministically; and the required caller-level, Docker, security-negative, and canary evidence is green.

